# YAML and TOML stay on git's own line merge, and the binding is load-bearing.
#
# mergiraf v0.18.0 resolves two sides that each append inside one YAML block
# scalar by keeping ours and DROPPING theirs: it reports `Solved 1 conflict`
# and exits 0, so `git merge` prints `Auto-merging` and the incoming line is
# gone with nothing in the diff to show it. `run: |` is the commonest shape in
# this repository's workflows. On TOML it writes a DUPLICATE table and reports
# the merge solved — agent-glovebox PR #4569 emitted `[project]` twice, once
# per side, which no TOML parser accepts. Git's line merge conflicts loudly on
# both inputs, which is why they keep it.
#
# BOUND to `merge=text`, never merely left out of the list above.
# `gitattributes(5)`: an unspecified merge attribute takes its driver from the
# `merge.default` config variable, so on a checkout that sets
# `merge.default=mergiraf` deleting the pattern alone still routes YAML through
# it. `merge=text` names the built-in driver, which no config can redirect.
#
# The four patterns above are NOT deleted: that list is the verbatim
# `mergiraf languages --gitattributes` output, refreshed by hand when
# MERGIRAF_VERSION moves, and removing members breaks the next refresh.
# These win by position instead — git applies the LAST matching pattern.
#
# This exclusion is scoped to the git merge DRIVER. `mergiraf solve`, which
# auto-resolve/prepare.sh runs, rebuilds from conflict markers rather than
# three blobs and reads no attribute at all — auto-resolve/lib.sh's
# `structural_merge_unsafe` is what excludes these types there.
*.yml merge=text
*.yaml merge=text
*.toml merge=text

# Lockfiles are derived artifacts — never let git line-merge them into an
# inconsistent state (one that matches neither parent's manifest).
#
# THIS BLOCK MUST STAY BELOW THE MERGIRAF PATTERNS AND THE BLOCK-SCALAR
# EXCLUSIONS. `merge` is a single attribute and git applies the LAST matching
# pattern, so `*.json merge=mergiraf` placed after this would re-enable merging
# of package-lock.json, and `*.yaml` — under either driver — would do the same
# to pnpm-lock.yaml. That is
# also why `go.sum` and `go.work.sum` appear twice: mergiraf's verbatim list
# names them, and these lines deliberately win. Do not "fix" the contradiction
# by deleting either side.
uv.lock          -merge
pnpm-lock.yaml   -merge
package-lock.json -merge
yarn.lock        -merge
Cargo.lock       -merge
poetry.lock      -merge
Gemfile.lock     -merge
go.sum           -merge
go.work.sum      -merge

# plugin/dist is a build output committed for the plugin loader. `-merge` refuses
# the line merge between two builds; regenerate instead. It sits LAST so it wins
# over the mergiraf patterns above for every file type it contains.
plugin/dist/** -merge linguist-generated=true
